Subject: wasix: do not interrupt a syscall that cannot block

__asyncify polls the work once and, when it is still pending and the
thread has signals queued, runs their handlers and returns Intr,
dropping the work. Host file operations go to Tokio's blocking pool, so
their first poll is always pending: a file syscall that runs through
__asyncify fails with EINTR whenever a signal is queued while it is in
flight. That is the answer a call the guest may block in owes its
caller, as a socket read, a wait or a sleep does, applied to calls that
cannot block, where the guest has no reason to expect it and no way to
tell a spurious failure from a real one.

POSIX gives lseek no EINTR at all: it does not block, so a caller may
take its answer as final, and a caller that measures a file by seeking
to its end therefore never retries. Under WASIX such a caller was told
the file had no size whenever a signal arrived during the seek, which a
parallel index build in a guest database server, whose workers signal
the leader while it extends the relation it is filling, hit in most
runs. fsync and fdatasync may return EINTR by the letter of POSIX, but
a handler installed with SA_RESTART restarts them, nothing in a host
flush waits on the guest, and Intr here abandons the flush while the
host may still perform it, so the guest is told its data is unsynced
with no way to learn otherwise.

Give the asyncify machinery a second entry point for work that cannot
block. It runs the queued handlers exactly as before, so a handler that
exits the process still ends the call, and then polls the work again
instead of abandoning it, going round until the queue is empty and the
waker is subscribed again, so the syscall returns the answer it was
asked for. The callers that take it are fd_seek's seek to the end of a
file, fd_sync and fd_datasync, each of which reaches __asyncify only
for a file handle.

This is not general syscall restart. The sync calls complete whether
or not the handler was installed with SA_RESTART, which a caller
cannot tell from a sync that finished before the signal arrived, and
no other call is restarted.

Everything else keeps the interruptible entry point. sock_send_file
reads a file too, but the call it is part of writes that file to a
socket and blocks for as long as the socket takes, so it is a call the
guest may block in and EINTR is its caller's to expect; the same goes
for name resolution, process spawning and the network-port calls. Reads
and writes are not affected either way, since they go through the light
asyncify, which never looks at the signal queue; opening a path,
stat-ing one, reading a directory and unlinking do not asyncify at all.

diff --git a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/mod.rs b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/mod.rs
index a80e76e..2ec5670 100644
--- a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/mod.rs
+++ b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/mod.rs
@@ -307,11 +307,48 @@ where
 /// thus allowed for asynchronous operations to execute. It has built in functionality
 /// to (optionally) timeout the IO, force exit the process, callback signals and pump
 /// synchronous IO engine
+///
+/// The work is abandoned and `Errno::Intr` returned when a signal is queued
+/// while it is still pending, which is what a call the guest may block in
+/// owes its caller. Use [`__asyncify_uninterruptible`] for work that cannot
+/// block, where `Errno::Intr` would be a failure the guest has no way to
+/// have expected.
 pub(crate) fn __asyncify<T, Fut>(
     ctx: &mut FunctionEnvMut<'_, WasiEnv>,
     timeout: Option<Duration>,
     work: Fut,
 ) -> WasiResult<T>
+where
+    T: 'static,
+    Fut: std::future::Future<Output = Result<T, Errno>>,
+{
+    __asyncify_maybe_interruptible(ctx, timeout, work, true)
+}
+
+/// As [`__asyncify`], for work that cannot block.
+///
+/// Queued signals still have their handlers run while the work is pending,
+/// but the work is then polled again rather than abandoned, so the call
+/// returns what it was asked for instead of `Errno::Intr`. A handler that
+/// exits the process still ends the call, as it does everywhere else.
+pub(crate) fn __asyncify_uninterruptible<T, Fut>(
+    ctx: &mut FunctionEnvMut<'_, WasiEnv>,
+    timeout: Option<Duration>,
+    work: Fut,
+) -> WasiResult<T>
+where
+    T: 'static,
+    Fut: std::future::Future<Output = Result<T, Errno>>,
+{
+    __asyncify_maybe_interruptible(ctx, timeout, work, false)
+}
+
+fn __asyncify_maybe_interruptible<T, Fut>(
+    ctx: &mut FunctionEnvMut<'_, WasiEnv>,
+    timeout: Option<Duration>,
+    work: Fut,
+    interruptible: bool,
+) -> WasiResult<T>
 where
     T: 'static,
     Fut: std::future::Future<Output = Result<T, Errno>>,
@@ -330,6 +367,7 @@ where
     {
         ctx: &'a mut FunctionEnvMut<'b, WasiEnv>,
         pinned_work: Pin<Box<Fut>>,
+        interruptible: bool,
     }
     impl<Fut, T> Future for SignalPoller<'_, '_, Fut, T>
     where
@@ -337,23 +375,38 @@ where
     {
         type Output = Result<Fut::Output, WasiError>;
         fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {
-            if let Poll::Ready(res) = Pin::new(&mut self.pinned_work).poll(cx) {
-                return Poll::Ready(Ok(res));
-            }
-            if let Some(signals) = self.ctx.data().thread.pop_signals_or_subscribe(cx.waker()) {
+            loop {
+                if let Poll::Ready(res) = Pin::new(&mut self.pinned_work).poll(cx) {
+                    return Poll::Ready(Ok(res));
+                }
+                let Some(signals) = self.ctx.data().thread.pop_signals_or_subscribe(cx.waker())
+                else {
+                    // The queue was empty, so the waker above is subscribed
+                    // and a later signal will poll us again.
+                    return Poll::Pending;
+                };
                 if let Err(err) = WasiEnv::process_signals_internal(self.ctx, signals) {
                     return Poll::Ready(Err(err));
                 }
-                return Poll::Ready(Ok(Err(Errno::Intr)));
+                if self.interruptible {
+                    return Poll::Ready(Ok(Err(Errno::Intr)));
+                }
+                // The work cannot block, so it is still owed an answer. Go
+                // round again: the poll above resubscribes the waker once the
+                // queue is empty, and picks up any signal that arrived while
+                // the handlers ran.
             }
-            Poll::Pending
         }
     }
 
     // Block on the work
     let mut pinned_work = Box::pin(work);
     let tasks = env.tasks().clone();
-    let poller = SignalPoller { ctx, pinned_work };
+    let poller = SignalPoller {
+        ctx,
+        pinned_work,
+        interruptible,
+    };
     block_on_with_timeout(&tasks, timeout, poller)
 }
 
